Scheme name:

una

Status:

Provisional 

Applications/protocols that use this scheme name: 
una is the addressing scheme of the UNA dominion, the registry and document system operated by United Nations from America (UNA), a nation of nations with an enrolled membership. UNA's member application is the reference implementation. Any software that implements the published syntax is a permitted and invited use of the scheme. Programs that handle URIs can carry una:// addresses; an implementation without access to the registry can recognize, store, forward, and pass through them without alteration.

Scheme syntax:

una-URI = "una://" una-authority [ path-abempty ]

[ "?" query ] [ "#" fragment ]

una-authority = 1*( unreserved / pct-encoded / sub-delims )

The rules path-abempty, query, fragment, unreserved, pct-encoded, and sub-delims are defined in RFC 3986. The scheme name is case-insensitive; lowercase "una" is the preferred form.

Example:

una://UNA.UNA

Scheme semantics:

A una:// address designates a location within the UNA dominion, the registry and document system operated by UNA. The authority component carries the registry path of the record being addressed. Addresses are resolved within the registry by an application registered as the handler for the scheme, and the record to which the address points is presented to the user. Addresses are not resolved through the public Domain Name System.

Encoding considerations:

Characters outside the unreserved set are percent-encoded in accordance with RFC 3986. Text values are encoded in UTF-8 before percent-encoding. The scheme carries no binary content.

Interoperability considerations:

Addresses are resolved within the UNA registry and are not resolved through the public Domain Name System, so no DNS records are involved. An implementation without access to the registry can still recognize, store, forward, and pass through una:// addresses without alteration. The syntax follows the generic URI syntax of RFC 3986, so the scheme composes with URI processing in existing software. The scheme name is case-insensitive. The domain-based form com.unarepublic.una:// identifies the same dominion for applications that follow the domain-based naming convention for private schemes.

Security considerations:

Any application can register as a handler for the scheme, so users should confirm which application their device uses to open una:// addresses. Applications that display records should verify the authenticity of the record source before relying on its contents. UNA does not assert that a third-party implementation is authorized, endorsed, or operated by UNA, and an implementation represents itself as doing so only with written authorization from UNA. Confidential member records are not published through the scheme.

